      <ArticleTitle>Diagnostic Performance of Radiologists for Acute Intracranial Hemorrhage on Noncontrast Head <i>CT</i>: A Multicenter Reader Study.</ArticleTitle>
      <Pagination><MedlinePgn>345-53</MedlinePgn></Pagination>
      <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1148/radiol.2021000001</ELocationID>
      <Abstract>
        <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Missed intracranial hemorrhage (ICH) on head CT remains a source of diagnostic error.</AbstractText>
        <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In this retrospective multicenter reader study, 12 board-certified radiologists independently interpreted 1,250 consecutive noncontrast head CT scans (n = 1,250) from three hospitals.</AbstractText>
        <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Pooled reader sensitivity was 88.4% (95% CI, 85.1%–91.2%) and specificity was 93.0% (95% CI: 91.0–94.7). The area under the curve was 0.91 (0.89–0.93). Accuracy of 91.5% was observed.</AbstractText>
        <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Unaided radiologist performance provides a benchmark for future computer-aided detection studies.</AbstractText>
      </Abstract>
